{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfgSZ4k4Q3brADCo9dJHw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate to `iter_2/candidate_5/`, and the self-check printed `OK`. The fit has not yet been checked for convergence, speed or novelty — admission tests those.\n\n**Model chosen:** `rule_built_tally_switch_triplet` (rank 4, 128 nats behind the best). It is the parent of the current best model, `person_pattern_sensitivity_rule_built`. Two other models refined this same parent: that best model, which added a personal sensitivity to pattern structure, and `person_decisiveness_rule_built_tally`, which added a personal decisiveness on the whole impression. Both changed how strongly each person reacts. Neither changed the direction of anyone's preference.\n\n**Name:** `personal_switch_ideal_rule_built_tally`\n\n**The one change:** each person now has their own ideal switching rate, drawn from a population distribution (`sigmoid(mu_sw + sigma_sw·z_sw)`, 400 slots), instead of one rate shared by everyone. Some people expect a random coin to switch sides about half the time; others expect it to switch far more often. On pairs that differ only in how often they switch, people can therefore prefer opposite sequences rather than all leaning the same way with different strengths. This targets the critique's only flagged statistic: on pairs where pattern structure cannot act, people differ more in how often they agree with the majority than the best model allows. It is only a raw-p hit (p = 0.04, q = 0.32), not one that survives the FDR. Everything else is unchanged from the parent: the personal tally-span ideal and length-scaled sensitivity, the personal switching weight, the chunk-variety reward, the rule-built penalty and the left/right lean.\n\n**Self-check result** (1 chain, 100 tuning + 100 draws): it loads, its log-probability is finite and it meets the data contract. Its ELPD-LOO on this short run is -3388.3, against -3413.6 for the parent and -3285.7 for the best model. Two caveats from that run:\n- PSIS-LOO was flagged unreliable: 12.7% of trials had a high Pareto k. That is usual for a 100-draw run, but the full fit has not shown that it goes away.\n- **Sampling time is a risk.** The short run took 215 s for 200 iterations. The production fit is 4 chains of 7,000 iterations each at target_accept 0.99, under a 30-minute limit per sampling run. At that rate it would run over, unless the check was slowed by sharing a single CPU, which I haven't confirmed. In its favour, the model has the same number of per-person vectors (five) as the best model, which did fit in time."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1288,"cache_read_input_tokens":98315,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1288,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d7cbc7af-a831-4368-9f27-de7caf7f9e9b","uuid":"381d3a07-f3cd-48e0-8086-4bf49aaeefb6","timestamp":"2026-10-04T02:53:53.086Z","request_id":"req_011CfgSZ4J1kehkbDD5rCcTP"}
